Trace:

  1. _lpTokenValue() is internal view and is reached only via _calculateBaseToQuote(), which is the price-read path.
  Nothing in this path takes attacker-supplied input: LP_TOKEN, PRIMARY_INDEX, SECONDARY_INDEX, TOKEN_1/2, DECIMALS_1/2,
  dyAmount, baseToUSDOracle, and invertBase are all immutable, fixed at construction. There is no setter, no
  permissioned upgrade of these fields, and no external entry point that lets an unprivileged caller change which pool
  or which indices are used.
  2. The only way to reach the claimed state — an oracle whose balances(0)+balances(1) do not span the pool — is for
  whoever deploys the oracle to pass a 3-coin pool address as _lpToken to a contract literally named Curve2TokenOracle,
  whose entire ABI surface (coins(0), coins(1), SECONDARY_INDEX = 1 - PRIMARY_INDEX, length-2 arrays throughout)
  declares its domain as two-token pools. That is a deployment/configuration act performed by the protocol's trusted
  deployer/governance, and registering the resulting oracle for a vault is likewise a privileged listing action. An
  external unprivileged attacker cannot cause this construction, cannot select the pool, and cannot cause a
  correctly-deployed 2-coin oracle to start reading a 3-coin pool.
  3. So the "exploit" reduces to: if governance misdeploys a two-token oracle onto a three-token pool, prices are wrong.
  That is a trusted-role misconfiguration precondition, not an attacker-controlled path. The liquidator's ~33% discount
  only materializes downstream of that admin error; the liquidator is exercising ordinary liquidation logic against a
  price that a privileged actor set up incorrectly. Under the judging rule, an exploit whose enabling step is a
  privileged actor acting (or erring) within its own deployment permissions is out of scope.
  4. Independently, the claim cannot be confirmed against the shown code: _calculateLPTokenValue lives in
  AbstractLPOracle and is not provided. The constructor also stores _lowerLimitMultiplier/_upperLimitMultiplier and the
  base class performs a spot-vs-oracle deviation check with them; whether that check, or any require inside
  _calculateLPTokenValue, would revert on the mispriced input cannot be verified from the material given. Nor is any
  liquidation contract shown, so the "seize healthy positions at a fabricated discount" step is asserted rather than
  traced. I cannot point to the exact line where an invariant breaks for an unprivileged caller.
  5. Also worth noting: nothing about a 3-coin pool causes a silent arithmetic fault here at all — the reads succeed and
  the math is internally consistent for the two coins it does read. The defect, if any, is purely "wrong pool wired
  in," i.e. a configuration mismatch, which is the definition of an admin-scoped issue.

  Both the privileged-precondition rule and the tie-break (missing _calculateLPTokenValue / missing liquidation code)
